Institutional identity
The temple carries two public-facing inscriptions:
南海觀音寺 (Nánhǎi Guānyīn Sì)
  — "South Sea Guanyin Temple"
  — the institutional name, identifying the temple
    through Guanyin's mythological homeland:
    Potalaka (普陀山), the island paradise in the
    South Seas that is her Pure Land

南海飛來 (Nánhǎi Fēi Lái)
  — "Flew here from the South Sea"
  — a founding narrative inscription asserting that the
    temple's principal deity image miraculously arrived
    from Potalaka itself — a common legitimating claim
    in Chinese temple founding traditions
The double inscription establishes this as a temple whose entire identity is anchored in a specific tradition of Guanyin veneration — the South Sea / Potalaka lineage — and whose founding authority rests on a narrative of miraculous divine origination rather than human founding alone.
This is a small, single-hall community temple, structurally distinct from the large monastic compounds analyzed previously. The entire iconographic programme is concentrated within one hall, with the threshold as the only spatial boundary between outside and inside.
Zone 1 — Threshold
Image 1
ICON₁ — Budai (布袋, Cloth-Sack Maitreya)
(Exterior threshold, on granite pedestal)
ICON₁ → ⟨ threshold, singular, front-facing, devotional ⟩
The Budai figure is bronze, seated in the standard smiling posture on a lotus base mounted on a circular granite pedestal. He wears a fresh marigold garland — a South Asian / Theravada devotional decorative convention increasingly common in Singapore Chinese folk temples, signalling active and frequent tending by devotees. A coin slot is visible at the base of the pedestal, integrating donation function into the threshold figure's structure.
ENSEMBLE (threshold scope):
ENSEMBLE → [BUDAI – admits → TEMPLE_INTERIOR]
  [scope: shrine]

ENSEMBLE → [BUDAI – brackets → TEMPLE_ENTRANCE]
  [scope: shrine]
The Budai here performs a dual function consistent with the pattern documented across this corpus. As in the Hall of Celestial Kings at Lian Shan Shuang Lin and the Mahavira Hall at Kong Meng San, the welcoming Maitreya/Budai figure is positioned at the threshold to receive and admit the devotee before they encounter the principal devotional figure inside.
One thing worth noting: The marigold garland marks something specific. At the large monasteries in this corpus, threshold figures are maintained institutionally — dusted, incensed, offered to on a scheduled basis. At a small community temple like this one, the fresh garland signals that individual community members are actively attending to the deity's comfort in a more personal, immediate way. The threshold figure is devotionally alive, not merely architectural.
Zone 2 — Principal Altar
Image 2
ICONs
ICON₁ — Thousand-Armed Avalokiteśvara (千手觀音, Qiānshǒu Guānyīn)
(Central, principal deity)
ICON₁ → ⟨ central and recessed, dominant, front-facing,
           soteriological and devotional and intercessory ⟩
The principal deity is a seated Thousand-Armed Avalokiteśvara in gilt/gold finish, positioned centrally within an ornate dark-wood architectural niche with carved gold ornamentation. The most iconographically distinctive feature is the solar/radiate mandorla — a halo of multiple rays emanating from behind the figure like a sun disc — rather than the standard leaf-shaped or oval mandorla typical in Chinese Mahāyāna Buddhist iconography.
This solar mandorla is analytically significant and warrants a provisional note:
Note on the solar mandorla:
  In standard Chinese Buddhist iconography, Avalokiteśvara
  typically appears with either an oval mandorla, a leaf-shaped
  mandorla, or no mandorla. A radiate solar halo is more
  commonly associated with:
    - Amoghapaśa Avalokiteśvara (不空羂索觀音) forms
    - Mahāvairocana Buddha (大日如來) in esoteric contexts
    - Local syncretic adaptations incorporating solar deity
      traditions

  Whether this reflects an esoteric Tantric iconographic
  lineage, a local Singapore Chinese folk synthesis, or
  an eclectic aesthetic choice requires further fieldwork
  and consultation with temple management.
  Flagged as provisional pending confirmation.
ICON₂ — Left Flanking Attendant
(Left of principal deity, slightly forward and below)
ICON₂ → ⟨ paired-lateral, subordinate, front-facing, intercessory ⟩
A smaller gilt figure, seated, to Avalokiteśvara's left (viewer's right). Provisional identification: possibly Sudhana (善財童子, "Good Wealth Boy"), one of Guanyin's two canonical attendants, though the image resolution does not permit definitive iconographic identification without fieldwork confirmation.
ICON₃ — Right Flanking Attendant
(Right of principal deity, slightly forward and below)
ICON₃ → ⟨ paired-lateral, subordinate, front-facing, intercessory ⟩
A smaller gilt figure, seated, to Avalokiteśvara's right (viewer's left). Provisional identification: possibly Longnü (龍女, "Dragon Girl"), the second canonical attendant of Guanyin. The pairing of Sudhana and Longnü as Guanyin's attendants is the canonical configuration in Chinese Mahāyāna Buddhist iconography.
ICON₄, ICON₅ — Lateral figures (partially visible)
Two larger dark-coloured figures are partially visible at the left and right edges of the altar space. Their identities cannot be determined from the available photographs. Flagged as requiring fieldwork confirmation.
