Naming conventions
The temple demonstrates two of the three naming conventions documented in this corpus:
南海觀音寺  — DEITY-NAMED
  The temple is named after its principal deity (Guanyin)
  qualified by her mythological homeland (South Sea).
  This follows the deity-named convention (e.g., 觀音殿,
  大雄宝殿) established across the corpus.

南海飛來  — DOCTRINALLY/NARRATIVELY NAMED
  The secondary inscription names the founding theological
  claim — miraculous arrival from Potalaka — encoding the
  temple's authority in the narrative of divine origination.
  This is a variant of the doctrinally named convention
  documented at 光明殿 (Universal Brightness) and
  無相殿 (No Form).
Comparison with Da Feng Gu Si (previous analysis)
Both temples are small single-hall community temples in Singapore, analyzed in this corpus. Comparing them is instructive:
DA FENG GU SI (南洋同奉善堂 / 大峰古寺)
  Principal cult: Master Dafeng (syncretic Buddhist patriarch)
  Flanking deities: Huatuo (Daoist physician) and
                    Hu Tian Yuanshuai (martial protector)
  Triad logic: functional welfare triad encoding the Shan Tang
               charitable programme (charity, medicine, protection)
  Buddhist frame: Theravada Buddha above, mantras on lanterns
  Syncretic mode: iconographic integration — Buddhist, Daoist,
                  and folk deities on the same altar, with
                  Buddhist frame surmounting syncretic content

NANHAI GUANYIN TEMPLE (南海觀音寺)
  Principal cult: Thousand-Armed Avalokiteśvara
                  (canonical Chinese Mahāyāna Buddhist)
  Flanking deities: probable Sudhana and Longnü
                    (canonical Guanyin attendants)
  Triad logic: devotional triad — principal bodhisattva
               with paired canonical attendants
  Syncretic mode: primarily Buddhist, with possible
                  folk synthesis in the solar mandorla
                  (provisional, requiring confirmation)
Da Feng Gu Si encodes a functionally syncretic institutional theology in which Buddhist, Daoist, and folk deities are integrated within a Buddhist frame. Nanhai Guanyin Temple, by contrast, presents a more internally consistent Mahāyāna Buddhist iconographic programme, with the solar mandorla as the primary marker of possible folk or esoteric influence.
Methodological notes
On the solar mandorla: This analysis flags it as provisionally unresolved. Following RFIG's limitation notice that "it is recommended to use RFIG as a supplementary tool, in addition to academic literature and religious texts," the correct next step is consultation with the temple's management about the specific iconographic tradition the principal image represents, supplemented by checking relevant scholarship on Thousand-Armed Guanyin variants in Southeast Asian Chinese Buddhism.
On the lateral figures (ICON₄ and ICON₅): The two larger dark-coloured figures partially visible at the edges of the altar cannot be identified from the available photographs. A proper in-person visit would document these and potentially significantly change the analysis of the altar's full iconographic programme. The RFIG guidelines' note on position is directly relevant: "Many Chinese folk temples may keep many deity figures in their shrines in all sorts of positions that may or may not reflect the original altar configuration. Therefore, use RFIG as a supplementary tool and make sure to understand more about the background of the shrines and temples from the management themselves."
On scale: This is the first small single-hall urban community temple in the corpus to receive a full RFIG analysis with both threshold and principal altar documented. Together with the Da Feng Gu Si analysis, it begins to establish a comparative small-temple baseline that contrasts instructively with the large monastery analyses. RFIG operates consistently across both scales with the same relational vocabulary.
